# Coach speech (Japanese): the review of the player's moves (src/coach/review.cpp, review_explain.cpp).
# Same keys and variants as assets/coach/speech/en/review.lang.
#
# Register: polite です/ます style, warm at levels 1-2, short noun-ending sentences (体言止め) at
# levels 5-6, never plain casual verb endings. The coach is 私.
# Placeholders (see the English header): {your} {my} = "あなたのナイト" / "私のビショップ";
# {your:bare} {my2:bare} {t2:bare} = the piece name alone, used when the owner is already clear
# from the sentence ("{t1}と{t2:bare}" = "あなたのクイーンとルーク": both targets of a fork, a
# skewer or a pin always belong to the same side). {sq} = a square: "{sq}へ", "{sq}の", "{sq}で",
# never "{sq}に" (rank 2 is spoken "に"). Moves and lines are noun phrases in speech
# ("ナイト・エフさん"), so particles follow them: "{best}が良い手でした", "{line}でメイト".
# Counted numbers: {m:move} "3手", {pts:point} "3点", {n:square} "3マス", {n:time} "3回",
# {n:ko} "3個" (count.ko in common.lang). A ".spoken" key respells for the voice what it may
# misread: digits and chess kanji in kana (大悪手 だいあくしゅ, 疑問手 ぎもんしゅ, 妙手
# みょうしゅ, 駒得 こまどく, 両取り りょうどり, 端 はし ...).
#
# Chess words (glossary): fork フォーク, double attack 両取り, pin ピン, skewer スキュア,
# discovered attack ディスカバードアタック, back rank バックランク, passed pawn パスポーン,
# opposition オポジション, bishop pair ビショップペア, pawn ending ポーンエンディング,
# sacrifice サクリファイス (levels 1-2: 駒を捨てる / 捨て駒), brilliant move 妙手, great move 好手,
# blunder 大悪手, mistake 悪手 / ミス, inaccuracy 疑問手, loose / hanging piece 浮き駒 (levels 1-2:
# 守りのない駒, ただで取れる), mate in N "N手でメイト".

# ---- Announcements (either side's move) -------------------------------------------------------
# The player gives check (levels 1-2: say what it means).
